A line ending in `:=` or in a binary operator continues on the next line:
no `;` is forced and the next line takes one continuation level
(fmt.indent_style issue #89, fmt.semicolon_enforcement). The source
breaks are kept: the wrapper never moves a trailing operator to the
start of the next line (fmt.max_line_length "never moves or joins a
line break the source already has"). The `1` line
lacks its `;` and the next line is a statement, so one is added.
-- input.ssl --
:PROCEDURE Compute;
:PARAMETERS nBase, nRate;
:DECLARE nTotal, bReady;
nTotal :=
nBase * nRate;
bReady := nTotal > 0 .AND.
nRate < 1;
nTotal := nTotal -
1
:RETURN nTotal;
:ENDPROC;
-- format --
:PROCEDURE Compute;
	:PARAMETERS nBase, nRate;
	:DECLARE nTotal, bReady;

	nTotal :=
		nBase * nRate;
	bReady := nTotal > 0 .AND.
		nRate < 1;
	nTotal := nTotal -
		1;

	:RETURN nTotal;
:ENDPROC;
-- diagnostics --
10:1-10:8 error unexpected_token: Unexpected keyword ':RETURN' - expected an operator or ';' (is the previous statement missing its ';'?)
-- diagnostics [--strict --info] --
10:1-10:8 error unexpected_token: Unexpected keyword ':RETURN' - expected an operator or ';' (is the previous statement missing its ';'?)
